a media production lab for documentary filmmaking

ArcherGate Experiment 001
========================

The experiment makes two short edits from three labeled synthetic clips.
A plays Signal, Transit, then Echo. B plays Echo, Signal, then Transit.
B also shifts the source frame ranges for Echo and Transit. Each clip has
its own color, moving marker and synthesized tone.

Run the demonstration
---------------------

Open a terminal at the repository root on the experiment branch. Setup
targets an Ubuntu 24.04 x86-64 Codespace with Python 3.14 and Node.js 24
or newer. It downloads pinned FFmpeg, Python packages and browser test
tools into this checkout. The downloads need internet access. Setup uses
no sudo, system package installation, paid service or GPU.

    ./scripts/setup
    .venv/bin/python -m archergate demo

The second command generates the three sources, registers them with their
metadata and writes two playable MP4 files:

    workspace/renders/edit-a.mp4
    workspace/renders/edit-b.mp4

You can download either file from the Codespaces file explorer and open it
in a local media player. To watch and compare side by side:

    .venv/bin/python -m archergate serve --port 8000

In Codespaces, open the Ports tab, forward port 8000 if needed, confirm
its visibility is Private and open it in the browser. On a local machine,
open http://127.0.y0.1:8000. Use the two video players to play, pause and
seek. The rows below each player show cut order and source frame ranges.
Press Ctrl+C in the terminal to stop the preview.

Run the full acceptance checks after setup:

    ./scripts/verify

The suite generates its own media in temporary directories. It uses
ffprobe and FFmpeg to check and decode both exports, the official MCP SDK
to call all four tools over stdio, and Chrome for Testing to play and
seek both videos. The test needs local socket access for the browser.

Inspect and change an edit
--------------------------

    .venv/bin/python -m archergate assets
    .venv/bin/python -m archergate inspect experiments/001/edit-a.json
    .venv/bin/python -m archergate compare experiments/001/edit-a.json experiments/001/edit-b.json
    .venv/bin/python -m archergate render experiments/001/edit-b.json

The source registry is workspace/registry.json. A SHA-256 records each
source's exact bytes. The registry also stores where the clip came from,
its rights note, and its video and audio metadata. Rendering checks the
referenced hashes and replaces the export only after media validation.
The MP4's comment metadata embeds its manifest, source hashes and FFmpeg
version. The CLI prints the exported file's SHA-256.

Each JSON manifest lists cuts in playback order. Each cut references an
asset ID and a start and end frame. The start is included and the end is
excluded. At 24 fps, frames 12–60 yield 48 frames or two seconds. To try
a change, adjust one of the demo manifests, render it again and refresh
the page. To keep a separate export, copy a manifest, set a new ID and
pass its file path to the render command. For an independent workspace,
set --workspace /absolute/path before the subcommand and keep it outside
the checkout or under the ignored workspace directory.

You can register a locally held, rights-cleared clip by copying it to
workspace/sources/my-clip.mp4, then running:

    .venv/bin/python -m archergate register my-clip --provenance "Source and creation details" --rights "Permission or license details"

The clip must be a 640×360, 24 fps constant-rate MP4 with one video and
one audio stream starting at zero, with audio covering the video. Use
a new asset ID when a clip or its provenance changes. Retain the source
bytes, registry and manifests together to reproduce an editorial variant.
Keep sensitive source media and provenance records in private storage.

MCP
---

The local stdio server exposes inspect_assets, inspect_edit, compare_edits
and render_edit. Edit tools accept edit-a and edit-b. Render returns the
export path relative to the workspace, the manifest and content hashes.

Configure a local MCP client to launch the checkout's absolute
.venv/bin/python path with the arguments -m archergate.mcp_server and
run it from the repository root. If the client uses another working
directory, set its PYTHONPATH to this checkout's absolute path. Generate
the demo before inspecting the registry or requesting a render. To run
the repository's real MCP client check directly:

    .venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_mcp.py -v

Current limits
--------------

The browser and MCP expose the two named demo exports. The CLI can render
other manifests with up to 24 hard cuts and 60 seconds of material. The
server is a local preview with no authentication. The browser test uses
Ubuntu 24.04 runtime libraries unpacked into the workspace. Comparable
source assets and cut order are reproducible, and encoded bytes can vary
across hosts, font sets and FFmpeg builds.

The plan and observed verification results are in docs/experiment-001-plan.md.

